    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Cūḷavaṃsa</a>, Kashyapa seized the throne in 477 CE, moved his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> to the rock of Sigiriya and built his palace on its summit.</li><li>After Kashyapa's defeat in 495 CE the capital returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> and Sigiriya became a Buddhist monastery complex that lasted until the 13th or 14th century.</li><li>Its mirror wall carries more than 1,500 poems written by visitors, the only evidence of poetry found from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> period.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The mirror wall was once so reflective that the king could see himself as he walked alongside it, and one of the verses scribbled on it, deciphered by Senarath Paranavithana, reads: &quot;I am Budal. Came with hundreds of people to see Sigiriya. Since all the others wrote poems, I did not!&quot;</p></aside>
    </section>

