    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sigiriya lies in the northern Matale District near the town of Dambulla in the Central Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where a massive column of granite about 180 metres high rises abruptly from the low-lying plains that extend from the central mountain range. It is an inselberg, a hardened granite outcrop derived from a solidified magma plug of a former volcano, and Pidurangala Rock, one kilometre to the north, has a similar origin. The area was probably inhabited since prehistoric times: the Aligala rock shelter east of the rock indicates occupation around 3000 BCE, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> Period.</p><p>Brahmi inscriptions on the western side show that the many rock shelters and caves nearby were occupied by Buddhist monks and ascetics from as early as the 1st or 2nd century BCE. Monastic settlements were established in the 3rd century BCE on the western and northern slopes of the boulder-strewn hills. The shelters were made under large boulders, with carved drip ledges around the cave mouths, and inscriptions beside the ledges record their donation to the Buddhist monastic order as residences. These date between the 3rd century BCE and the 1st century CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sigiriya consists of an ancient citadel and was both a palace and a fortress. The ruins include an upper palace on the flat top of the rock, with cisterns cut into the rock, a mid-level terrace that holds the Lion Gate and the mirror wall with its frescoes, and lower palaces clinging to the slopes below. The moats, walls and gardens extended for a few hundred metres from the base of the rock. On a small plateau about halfway up the rock stood a gateway in the form of an enormous lion, from which the name Sinhagiri, the Lion Rock, derives. A sculpted lion's head once stood above the legs and paws flanking the entrance, but the head collapsed years ago.</p><p>The site plan combined symmetry and asymmetry. On the west side of the rock lay a park for the royals laid out on a symmetrical plan, with water-retaining structures and hydraulic systems, some of which work today. The south holds a man-made reservoir. The gardens fall into three linked forms: water gardens, cave and boulder gardens, and terraced gardens. Five gates were placed at the entrances, and the more elabourate western gate is thought to have been reserved for the royals.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Cūḷavaṃsa</a>, an ancient Sri Lankan chronicle, describes the making of Sigiriya. It records that in 477 CE Kashyapa I, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhatusena">Dhatusena</a> by a non-royal consort, seized the throne in a coup assisted by Migara, the king's nephew and army commander. Kashyapa murdered his father by walling him up alive and usurped a throne that rightfully belonged to his half-brother Moggallana, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a> and vowed revenge.</p><p>Expecting Moggallana's return, Kashyapa moved his capital and residence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> to the more secure summit of Sigiriya. During his reign, 477 to 495 CE, Sigiriya was developed into a complex city and fortress, and most of the elabourate constructions on the summit and around it, including defensive structures, palaces and gardens, date from this period. According to the chronicle, the area had been a large forest that became a hill after storms and landslides. Alternative stories name Dhatusena as the primary builder, with Kashyapa finishing the work in honour of his father, and still other accounts describe Kashyapa as a playboy king whose pleasure palace this was.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves.jpg" alt="Ajanta Caves" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_(63).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Soman, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The frescoes were praised by John Still, who wrote in 1907 that "the whole face of the hill appears to have been a gigantic picture gallery" and "the largest picture in the world perhaps". The paintings would have covered most of the western face of the rock, an area 140 metres long and 40 metres high, and graffiti refer to 500 ladies in them. Although classified as belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> period, their style is considered unique, with lines that enhance the sense of volume of the figures. The identity of the ladies has not been confirmed: some believe they were the ladies of the kings, others that they were women taking part in religious observances. The pictures resemble paintings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Visitors from the 8th century onward scribbled verses on the mirror wall, most of them in the 9th and 10th centuries. People of all walks of life wrote there, from poets to provincial governors to housewives. Of more than 1,500 poems, most are addressed to the ladies of the frescoes: men praised their beauty and women shared their envy. Senarath Paranavithana, the Archaeological Commissioner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, deciphered 685 verses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When Moggallana arrived he declared war and defeated Kashyapa in 495 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Cūḷavaṃsa</a> and folklore say that Kashyapa's army abandoned him after misreading a change of course by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephant</a> as a retreat, and that he took his own life with a blade; in other versions he is assassinated by poison administered by a concubine. Moggallana returned the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> and converted Sigiriya into a Buddhist monastery complex, which survived until the 13th or 14th century. No records are found from then until the 16th and 17th centuries, when it was used briefly as an outpost of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>.</p><p>In 1831 Major Jonathan Forbes of the 78th (Highlanders) Regiment of Foot, returning on horseback from Pollonnuruwa, encountered the brushwood-covered summit. Antiquarians and later archaeologists took an interest, and work began on a small scale in the 1890s, with H.C.P. Bell the first to research the site extensively. On 14 October 1967 paint was splashed on the frescoes; Luciano Maranzi, trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, assisted the restoration, which ended on 11 April 1968. A report presented in 2010 suggested that the 22 frescoes have been fading since 1930, and writing on the mirror wall is now banned.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sigiriya is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and is regarded as one of the best preserved examples of ancient urban planning. It is considered one of the most important urban planning sites of the first millennium, with a plan described as very elabourate and imaginative. The Cultural Triangle Project, launched by the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, turned its attention to Sigiriya in 1982, and archaeological work began on the entire city for the first time under it.</p><p>Its gardens are counted among the oldest landscaped gardens in the world. Senake Bandaranayake identifies three traditions of landscape gardening there: the symmetrical water gardens, the asymmetrical cave and boulder gardens, and the terraced gardens at the base of the rock; he suggests that the palace garden on the summit combines all three. Interpretations of the site differ. Some regard it as the work of a Buddhist community without a military function, and it may have been important in the competition between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist traditions in ancient Sri Lanka. Its fountains of circular limestone plates, fed by underground conduits, still work, especially in the rainy season.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sigiriya, the Lion Rock, is a granite column in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> that King Kashyapa made his fortress capital from 477 CE. After his defeat in 495 it became a Buddhist monastery. Its frescoes, mirror wall poems and gardens survive and are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

